namespace Osysharp.Charts;

// ── the shapes an author fills ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/// What a mark draws as. A closed vocabulary, like `AxisSide` and `LegendPos` beside it.
///
/// An enum rather than a string, so the compiler checks decisions about a mark's kind. `IsBarKind` switches on it
/// with no `_` arm, so a new member fails the build there, at the site that has to decide about it, instead of
/// silently drawing nothing.
///
/// `Candle` is the one member nothing compares against, and that is deliberate. Candle-specific data lives on
/// `CandleMark` and is selected by type, so this member's job is to keep a candle from matching any of the five
/// style passes. Without it a `CandleMark` would keep the inherited `Line` default and the line pass would draw a
/// curve through the closes.
public enum MarkKind { Line, Area, Column, Bar, Scatter, Candle }

/// One plotted series. Heterogeneous by design: a chart mixes a column series with a line, so a chart holds a
/// `Mark[]` and each element is whatever it needs to be.
///
/// Two questions, two mechanisms. `Kind` answers "how does this draw": a closed vocabulary of styles, because a
/// `Column` and a `Bar` hold identical data and differ only in which way the chart is turned. A subtype answers
/// "what data does this carry", and exactly one mark needs one: `CandleMark` below. Modelling the styles as subtypes
/// would be five near-empty classes replacing an exhaustively checked enum; putting the candle's extra arrays on
/// `Mark` would give every line series three arrays it can never use.
public class Mark {
  /// What this series draws as — set by the config record you wrote, not by hand.
  public MarkKind Kind = MarkKind.Line;
  /// What the legend and the accessible name call this series. Required: a series nobody can name is a colour.
  public string Label;
  /// The values, one per category, in the same order as the chart's `labels`. For a CANDLE this is the CLOSE — see
  /// `CandleMark`.
  public double[] Values;
  /// Override the palette slot. Null takes this mark's position in the fixed order.
  ///
  /// Set it explicitly when the series set changes at runtime (a filter, a picker). Colour should follow the entity,
  /// not its rank: with slots assigned by position, removing series 2 repaints series 3 to 8, and a reader who had
  /// learned "orange is EMEA" is now reading a different chart.
  public string? Color;
}

/// Open-high-low-close sessions: the one mark whose data shape differs, and therefore the one that is a subtype.
///
/// Four numbers per category and one object rather than four series: the body spans open→close and the wick spans
/// low→high, so it cannot be assembled from four independent `Line`s.
///
/// `Values` (inherited) is the close, which is semantically right: the value of a trading day is its close. So the
/// scale, the hover readout, the legend, the accessible name and a moving-average `Line` drawn over the sessions all
/// work with no knowledge of candles. Only the places that need a high or a low ask for one, and they ask by type:
/// `marks.OfType<CandleMark>()`.
///
/// Three parallel arrays rather than an array of four-field records, because of the call site:
/// `Candle(opens: o, highs: h, lows: l, values: c)` is columnar, like `values: revenue` everywhere else, whereas a
/// record array would make every author write a per-session projection over four arrays they already hold.
///
/// Its `Kind` is always `MarkKind.Candle`, set by the `Candle(…)` record, which is the only thing that builds one.
/// The candle pass selects by type, so that member's job is to make a candle match none of the five style passes.
///
/// A consequence for anyone extending this kit: because this subtype exists, a `Mark[]` is not writable. A `Mark[]`
/// may really be a widened `CandleMark[]`, so `marks[i] = …` is a compile error naming this class (C# permits the
/// write and throws at run time; Osy# refuses it at compile time). Nothing in the kit writes through one. If you
/// need a writable sequence of marks, use a `List<Mark>`, which is invariant and therefore safe.
public class CandleMark : Mark {
  public double[] Opens;
  public double[] Highs;
  public double[] Lows;
}

/// Which edge an axis sits on. A closed vocabulary rather than a string, so `Axis(side: AxisSide.Left)` is checked and
/// completable — the same discipline `align: Align.Center` follows.
public enum AxisSide { Left, Bottom }

// ── annotations ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/// What an annotation draws as. A closed vocabulary, like `MarkKind`.
public enum AnnotationKind { Rule, Band, Note, Marker }

/// Something true about the chart that is not in the data: a target, an acceptable range, the day of a release.
///
/// An annotation is not a series. It has no palette slot, is absent from the legend, does not affect the scale and
/// cannot be toggled off, because none of those things is true of "the target is 60k". Modelling a target as a
/// one-value `Line` is the usual shortcut, and it costs exactly those four properties: the legend grows an entry
/// nobody clicks, the palette shifts under the real series, and the axis stretches to reach a number that was never
/// measured.
///
/// An annotation does not move the scale. A target of 500k on a chart topping out at 73k would otherwise squash
/// every real bar into the bottom eighth to make room for a line. Out-of-range annotations are clamped to the plot
/// edge and say so in their accessible name ("target 500,000 (above the top of this chart)"), and a clamped rule
/// also shows an arrow beside its label, so it does not look as if the target had been met.
///
/// One shape tagged with `Kind`, not the subtype treatment `Mark`/`CandleMark` got. A kind earns a type when it
/// carries data the others cannot: a candle has four numbers per category where every other mark has one. `Rule`,
/// `Band` and `Note` differ only in which of three small scalars they read, and the author never writes `Annotation`
/// directly (only the three records), so three near-empty classes would buy nothing.
public class Annotation {
  /// Which of the three this is — set by the config record you wrote, not by hand.
  public AnnotationKind Kind = AnnotationKind.Rule;
  /// What it is called, on the chart and in its accessible name. Empty draws the geometry unlabelled.
  public string Label = "";
  /// Override the annotation ink. Null takes the kit's annotation colour, which is deliberately NOT a palette slot:
  /// an annotation that borrows series 3's hue reads as series 3.
  public string? Color;

  /// `Rule` and `Note`: the value it sits at, on the value axis.
  public double Value;
  /// `Band`: the two edges. A band drawn from the larger to the smaller is the same band — they are sorted.
  public double From;
  public double To;
  /// `Note` and `Marker`: which category it sits on, by NAME rather than by index.
  ///
  /// By name because categories are runtime data: insert a month at the front and every index-addressed note would
  /// silently point one slot to the left. A name that no longer exists is a mismatch the chart can report, and does
  /// (see `AnnotationMarks` in Plot.osy).
  public string Category = "";
}

/// Where the legend sits, or that there is none.
///
/// `None` is a position, not a separate `show` flag: a boolean and a position would be two ways to say "no legend"
/// that could silently disagree.
public enum LegendPos { Top, Bottom, Left, Right, None }

/// The legend.
public class LegendDef {
  /// Which edge it sits on. `Bottom` is the default and is right for most charts: a legend above the plot separates
  /// the title from the picture it names.
  public LegendPos Position = LegendPos.Bottom;

  /// CLICK A SERIES TO HIDE IT, and click again to bring it back.
  ///
  /// Off by default, because an affordance that looks live must be live. A legend that responds to a click is a
  /// control, and on a chart printed into a report it promises something the reader cannot use. Turn it on where the
  /// chart is something people work with.
  ///
  /// The scale follows. Hiding the tallest series rescales the axis, which is what makes toggling useful: the remaining
  /// series expand to fill the plot instead of huddling at the bottom of a scale set by something no longer on screen.
  /// Colours do not follow: a series keeps its palette slot whether or not it is showing, so hiding series 2 does not
  /// repaint series 3 to 8 under a reader who had learned what orange meant.
  public bool Interactive = false;
}

/// One AXIS. A chart declares the axes it wants; none is a legitimate answer for a sparkline.
public class AxisDef {
  /// `Left` (the value axis) | `Bottom` (the category axis).
  ///
  /// You rarely declare `Bottom`: giving the chart `labels` is the request for a category axis. Declaring it is
  /// harmless.
  public AxisSide Side = AxisSide.Left;
  /// Draw this axis's gridlines. On by default for the value axis, which is where they earn their keep.
  public bool Grid = true;
  /// How many value ticks to aim for. The real count is chosen to land on ROUND numbers, so it may differ by one.
  public int Ticks = 4;

  /// What this axis MEASURES — "Revenue", "Users", "°C". Empty draws none.
  ///
  /// Rendered horizontally above the ticks rather than rotated up the side. A rotated title is the older convention
  /// and it is the harder one to read; putting it where a column heading would go costs no rotation and no width.
  public string Title = "";

  /// How every value on this axis is rendered — a .NET format string, spelled the way C# spells it: `"N0"`, `"P1"`,
  /// `"#,##0.00"`. Governs the tick labels, the hover readout, the on-mark values and every mark's accessible name at
  /// once, so a chart whose axis says `1.2K` and whose tooltip says `1234.0` is not expressible.
  ///
  /// Empty is the COMPACT default — `1.5K`, `2.4M`, `42` — which is what an axis usually wants and which no .NET
  /// specifier produces.
  public string Format = "";

  /// Wrapped around every formatted value — `Prefix = "£"`, `Suffix = " ms"`.
  ///
  /// Currency is usually better as a PREFIX than as the `C` specifier: `C` under the invariant culture renders the
  /// generic `¤` sign, because the invariant culture has no country. `Prefix = "£"` with `Format = "N0"` is what you
  /// meant. (`C` itself works, and formats in the browser like every other specifier.)
  public string Prefix = "";
  public string Suffix = "";

  /// An EXPLICIT domain. Null takes the value from the data — the default, and right nearly always.
  ///
  /// `Min` does not let a bar chart lie. With a column, bar or area on the chart the baseline stays at zero: a `Min`
  /// above zero is not applied, because starting the axis at the data's minimum exaggerates small differences and is
  /// the best-known way to mislead with a chart. A `Min` below zero still extends the axis downward. For a line,
  /// scatter or candle chart (a stock price, a temperature) `Min` moves the axis, where zooming is honest.
  public double? Min;
  /// The top of the scale, exactly. Null rounds up to the next round tick so the tallest mark meets a gridline.
  public double? Max;
}

// ── the config records — what a call site writes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Bodyless components whose base type names the shape they contribute. They are written as children of `Chart`,
// which reads as the picture being described rather than as an array literal being assembled:
//
//     Chart(title: "Revenue", labels: months) {
//       Column(label: "2024", values: last);
//       Line(label: "2025", values: current);
//       Axis(side: AxisSide.Left);
//     }

/// A connected line through the values. The default mark: it is what a time series is.
[AllowAnonymous] component Line(string label, double[] values, string? color = null, MarkKind kind = MarkKind.Line) : Mark;

/// A line with the space beneath it washed in the series hue. Use for ONE series, or for a total — overlapping
/// washes muddy into a colour that is neither series.
[AllowAnonymous] component Area(string label, double[] values, string? color = null, MarkKind kind = MarkKind.Area) : Mark;

/// Vertical bars from a single baseline — magnitude by category, the most-read chart there is.
[AllowAnonymous] component Column(string label, double[] values, string? color = null, MarkKind kind = MarkKind.Column) : Mark;

/// HORIZONTAL bars — the transpose of `Column`, and the right form whenever the category NAMES are long or there
/// are many of them: "Enterprise renewals" fits beside a bar and does not fit under a column, and twenty rows scroll
/// where twenty columns crush.
///
/// A `Bar` turns the whole chart; it is not a mark you mix in. The value axis moves to the bottom, the categories
/// run down the left and the gridlines stand vertical, so a `Bar` and a `Column` in one chart would need two
/// coordinate systems at once. The kit treats the chart as horizontal as soon as any mark is a `Bar`.
///
/// The axis follows the orientation: in a horizontal chart `Axis(side: AxisSide.Bottom)` is the VALUE axis (format, min/max,
/// ticks, title) and `Axis(side: AxisSide.Left)` names the categories — exactly the other way round from a column chart, and
/// exactly where each one is on the screen.
[AllowAnonymous] component Bar(string label, double[] values, string? color = null, MarkKind kind = MarkKind.Bar) : Mark;

/// Points without a connecting line. For a series whose x-positions are samples rather than a sequence.
[AllowAnonymous] component Scatter(string label, double[] values, string? color = null, MarkKind kind = MarkKind.Scatter) : Mark;

/// OPEN-HIGH-LOW-CLOSE candles — one object per session, not four series.
///
/// `values` is the CLOSE; `opens`, `highs` and `lows` are the other three, one per category, in the same order.
/// A `Line` on the same chart is the way to draw a moving average over them, and it needs nothing special: the
/// candle series' `values` are its closes, so both are on one scale and in one legend.
///
/// The axis is a session axis, not a clock. Give it `labels` of trading days rather than a proportional time scale:
/// that is what stops weekends opening gaps in the series, and it is what price charts conventionally do. The
/// category axis draws as many labels as fit, measured against the rendered width, so sixty sessions is fine.
///
/// Direction is both colour and shape. An up session is hollow and green, a down session filled and red, because
/// colour alone is not a channel for a reader with a colour-vision deficiency, on a greyscale print, or under
/// `forced-colors`. `color` overrides the outline for a chart that wants one hue.
[AllowAnonymous] component Candle(string label, double[] values, double[] opens, double[] highs, double[] lows,
                                  string? color = null, MarkKind kind = MarkKind.Candle) : CandleMark;

/// How much chart surrounds the marks.
///
/// `Bare` is the sparkline, and it is a mode rather than a second component. A sparkline is the same picture with
/// everything that is not a mark taken away: no card, no axes, no gridlines, no legend, no visible title and no hover
/// readout. A separate `Sparkline` component would be a second path through the same marks, and the two would drift.
///
/// A title is not dropped, it moves. `Chart(title: "Sales", chrome: Bare)` draws no heading and makes "Sales" the
/// chart's accessible name, so a sparkline still announces what it is.
///
/// An enum rather than `bare: true` because the card chrome is a `variants` dimension, and a variants dimension is
/// keyed by an enum-typed parameter.
public enum ChartChrome { Card, Bare }

/// Where the data TABLE goes, if the chart has one.
///
/// `Instead` is a first-class choice, not a fallback for a broken chart. Plenty of data is really a table: a few
/// numbers that do not trend, a set of unrelated magnitudes, anything a reader will want to copy out.
public enum TablePos { Below, Instead }

/// The same data as rows.
///
/// It is also the accessibility answer, and a better one than more `aria-label`s. Every mark already names itself
/// ("Revenue: £42,000"), which is right when a reader is on that mark and useless for the questions they actually
/// have: "which month was highest?", "what were the other five?". Those are answered by structure:
/// `UiRole.Grid` / `UiRole.GridRow` / `UiRole.ColumnHeader` / `UiRole.GridCell` let a screen reader read by position
/// and announce a cell with its column header, and let a sighted reader select and copy. The table reuses the marks'
/// own formatting (`Formatted`, the series' `Label`), so it cannot disagree with the picture.
public class TableDef {
  /// `Below` the chart (the default), or `Instead` of it.
  public TablePos Position = TablePos.Below;
}

/// The data table — `Table()`, or `Table(position: TablePos.Instead)`.
///
/// Written as a child of `Chart`, like an axis or a legend. It follows the LEGEND: a series switched off leaves the
/// table too, because the table is the same data, and a table disagreeing with the picture beside it is worse than
/// no table.
[AllowAnonymous] component Table(TablePos position = TablePos.Below) : TableDef;

/// The legend — `Legend(position: LegendPos.Right)`, `Legend(interactive: true)`, `Legend(position: LegendPos.None)`.
///
/// Written as a child of `Chart`, like an axis. A chart with no `Legend(…)` child gets the default: at the bottom,
/// not interactive, and only when there are two or more series (one series needs none — the title already names it).
[AllowAnonymous] component Legend(LegendPos position = LegendPos.Bottom, bool interactive = false) : LegendDef;

/// A REFERENCE LINE across the plot at one value — a target, a limit, a budget, last year's average.
///
///     Rule(value: 60000.0, label: "Target");
///
/// Drawn dashed, in the annotation ink rather than a palette hue, so it never reads as a series. It follows the
/// orientation: horizontal on a column chart, vertical on a `Bar` chart, because it always crosses the VALUE axis.
///
/// The label sits at the end of the line, clear of the plot edge.
[AllowAnonymous] component Rule(double value, string label = "", string? color = null,
                                AnnotationKind kind = AnnotationKind.Rule) : Annotation;

/// A SHADED BAND between two values — an acceptable range, a control limit, a forecast envelope.
///
///     Band(from: 55000.0, to: 65000.0, label: "Target range");
///
/// Drawn BEHIND the marks, washed at low opacity for the reason an `Area` is: opacity blends with whatever surface
/// is really there, so one declaration is right on a light theme and on a dark one.
[AllowAnonymous] component Band(double from, double to, string label = "", string? color = null,
                                AnnotationKind kind = AnnotationKind.Band) : Annotation;

/// A LABELLED POINT at one category and one value — "the release landed here", "this is the outlier".
///
///     Note(category: "Apr", value: 61000.0, label: "v2 launch");
///
/// The label picks its own side. It sits to the right of the dot unless it would not fit there, measured with
/// `Layout.TextWidth` against the plot's real width, so a note on the last category flips to the left instead of
/// being clipped by the plot edge. The category index alone cannot decide this: "v2 launch" and
/// "Q3 reforecast complete" need different amounts of room at the same position.
[AllowAnonymous] component Note(string category, double value, string label, string? color = null,
                                AnnotationKind kind = AnnotationKind.Note) : Annotation;

/// AN EVENT ON THE CATEGORY AXIS — a line straight across the plot at one category: a release, a migration, the day a
/// price changed. It marks WHEN something happened, where a `Rule` marks a VALUE.
///
///     Marker(category: "Oct 14", label: "0.9.16");
///
/// Drawn dashed in the annotation ink, from the plot's foot to its top, with its label at the top. It crosses the CATEGORY
/// axis, so it is vertical on a column or line chart and horizontal on a `Bar` chart — the opposite of a `Rule`.
///
/// ⚑ It names its category by NAME, like a `Note`, and a name the chart does not have is said on the chart rather than
/// dropped. Two events on one category are best written as ONE marker whose label names both: two lines drawn on the same
/// spot are one line with two labels fighting over it.
[AllowAnonymous] component Marker(string category, string label = "", string? color = null,
                                  AnnotationKind kind = AnnotationKind.Marker) : Annotation;

/// An axis. `Axis(side: AxisSide.Left)` is the value axis; `Axis(side: AxisSide.Bottom)` names the categories.
[AllowAnonymous] component Axis(AxisSide side = AxisSide.Left, bool grid = true, int ticks = 4,
                                string title = "", string format = "",
                                string prefix = "", string suffix = "",
                                double? min = null, double? max = null) : AxisDef;

// ── what the pieces pass each other ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/// The resolved chart: everything `Chart` works out, in one value, for every piece below it.
///
/// Not author-facing. An app writes `Chart(…) { Column(…); Axis(…); }` and never names this. It exists because the
/// chart's pieces form a deep chain (`Chart` → `ChartBody` → `Plot` → `HoverBand` → `HoverCell` → `Readout`) and
/// nearly every one of them needs most of the same facts. Passed individually, those facts would make for long
/// signatures, and adding one would mean editing every signature and call site along the chain.
///
/// A parameter object, deliberately, rather than an ambient provide/inject value. An ambient value would remove the
/// parameter from the signatures, and with it the only visible statement of what a piece depends on.
///
/// `Marks` is the full list, including hidden series. The scale is computed over the visible subset before this is
/// built (`Lo`/`Top`/`Ticks` already account for it), but the list itself stays whole because a palette slot follows
/// the series: hiding series 2 must not repaint series 3 to 8. `VisibleMarks(c.Marks, c.Hidden)` is how a drawing
/// pass gets the subset, and `SlotOf` is always asked of `Marks`.
public class ChartCtx {
  /// Every series as written, hidden ones included (see the note above).
  public Mark[] Marks;
  /// The category names, in order.
  public string[] Labels;
  /// Which series are switched off, by label.
  public string[] Hidden;
  /// The annotations, in the order written. Not in the scale and not in the legend — see `Annotation`.
  public Annotation[] Annotations;
  /// The VALUE axis in force — the one the author declared for the orientation's value side, or a default.
  public AxisDef Axis;

  /// The bottom of the painted scale.
  public double Lo;
  /// The top of the painted scale — the highest tick, so the tallest mark meets a gridline.
  public double Top;
  /// The tick VALUES, top-down (the paint order for a vertical axis).
  public double[] Ticks;
  /// The plot's height in px.
  public int Height;

  /// Is this chart transposed? True the moment any mark is a `Bar`.
  public bool Horizontal;
  /// Are the bar series stacked rather than grouped?
  public bool Stacked;
  /// Draw the gridlines.
  public bool ShowGrid;
  /// Print each value on its mark.
  public bool ShowValues;
  /// Stripped to just the marks — no card, no axes, no legend, no hover (`chrome: Bare`).
  public bool Bare;
  /// Draw the value-axis strip.
  public bool HasValueAxis;
  /// Draw the category strip.
  public bool HasCategoryAxis;
}
